dynarmic arm64 backend: compact per-block bookkeeping (Keyboard 72)

Translating Nintype's engine leaves about 148,000 blocks. AddressSpace kept a
200-byte EmittedBlockInfo per block inline in a robin_map (three containers
each), a std::map for reverse lookup, and a robin_set per link target, with
every table at most half full. On a test phone that was over 200 MB.

- Store only what relinking needs per block: its size and one flat list of
  block-link sites (target, offset, type). Relocations to prelude routines are
  only needed while a block is first linked.
- Keep fastmem patch sites in one table keyed by absolute host pc.
- Reverse lookup is a sorted vector: code is only appended until ClearCache.
- Back-references are vectors (each block registers once per target), and
  RelinkForDescriptor no longer inserts an empty entry for every new block.
- Raise max_load_factor of the large tables to 0.8.

Behavior is unchanged: device parity (19 cases, exact capture stream incl.
score bits, words, files), nin_invalidation_test with and without fastmem.
Applied at configure time to build-tree copies (app/src/main/cpp/CMakeLists.txt).

